ters of fact things that  we can agree upon now these these could  be matters of fact in the sense of you  know the truck is is white or I have one  piece of chalk in my hand things that we  can verify with our senses they might  also be what we could call psychological  or sociological facts where we say well  in this society what is considered fair  play or a justice is this right now  that's not saying that that justice  really is that it's just saying these  people think of things in this way so  disputes are always about those sorts of  matters and we can dispute about them  back and forth and back and forth what  we try to do is get the other person he  says to agree about empirical facts and  he's got some nice examples in here he  says that for example if we say that you  know thrift is a virtue and somebody  else says thrift is a vice what are what  are we going to do well we'll talk about  the nature of what we mean by thrift and  what a virtue would be and why it would  be you know considered to be a good  thing overall to be thrifty and we could  go on and on but it's always going to be  factual stuff with the value stuff sort  of being presupposed or driving the  inquiry he you know he's got a nice  example here says when someone disagrees  with us about the moral value of a  certain act  type of action we do admittedly resort  to argument and try to win them over to  our way of thinking but we do not  attempt to show by our arguments he has  the wrong ethical feeling that would be  the value part towards a situation whose  nature he is correctly apprehended what  we attempt to show is he's mistaken  about the facts of the case so here's  the examples we argue he has  misconceived the agents motive or that  he's misjudged the effects of the action  or it's probable effects in view of the  agent's knowledge or he's failed to take  into account the special circumstances  in which the agent was placed or we  employ more general arguments about the  effects